# Hypothesis & Success Metrics (Module 3)

> Scenario B, RouteLogic Velocity

## Pre-work · Hypothesis check
- **Role , who you are solving for (from M2):** A mid-size 3PL dispatcher responsible for keeping a live fleet routed correctly and knowing, in real time, where every stop stands.
- **Goal , what this user is ultimately trying to achieve:** To reassign routes and read accurate delivery status off a single trustworthy board, without a phone in the other hand.
- **Friction / moment of misery , the specific pain blocking their goal:** The board lies to them. A reassignment takes 8 to 15 minutes to reach the driver with no push notification (BUG-2044), and completed stops still read "in progress" for up to an hour (BUG-2072). Their verdict is the churn signal in miniature: "We keep a WhatsApp group as the real system" (UXR-02). The coordinator has already replaced the product with a manual workaround.
- **Current workaround , the external tool or manual process they rely on (M2):** A WhatsApp group as the de facto real-time system (UXR-02: "the real system"). Direct voice calls to the office or drivers when data is lost or ambiguous (UXR-03: Elena had to call the office to read them off a screen). Inbound driver texts they have to catch and log, because drivers push completion off-platform too (UXR-01: Diego "just texting my dispatcher instead").
- **Problem Hook , your one-sentence framing of the business crisis (M1):** We must solve the imminent churn of enterprise revenue to leaner competitors by addressing the administrative complexity and operational noise that forces frontline fleet coordinators into slow, manual workarounds.
- **Value Proposition , the outcome your initiative promised to deliver (M1):** For frontline fleet coordinators at enterprise accounts, we will deliver RouteLogic Velocity, a streamlined interface that eliminates operational noise and manual workarounds while preserving backend platform power, because key accounts are actively trialing leaner rivals, putting our largest enterprise contracts at immediate risk.

## Read your data snapshots (Scenario B · RouteLogic)

- **Snapshot 1 · Feature Adoption by Role , Does the low adoption confirm your persona is burdened by tools they don't use? Note whether the low scheduling adoption (42%) for coordinators matches your M2 moment of misery.:** Partly, and honestly the part that doesn't fit is the more useful finding. The low-adoption features (AI Predictive ETAs at 23% for coordinators and 11% for drivers, Shift Scheduling at 42%, Financial Reporting at 8%) back up my M1 point about admin bloat: RouteLogic keeps building headline features the frontline never touches, which is real wasted investment. But here's the pivot-check catch. My persona's moment of misery isn't in a feature she avoids. She lives in the two most-used tools on the board, the Live Dispatch Board (91%) and the Route Optimizer (85%), and that's exactly where my M2 friction sits. So the pain isn't "tools I don't use", it's that the tools she leans on most are the ones letting her down. That makes it more urgent, not less. The 42% scheduling number is a real secondary gap, but it isn't the daily wound. If I chased scheduling I'd be trimming bloat instead of fixing the core trust problem that's actually pushing her to WhatsApp.

- **Snapshot 2 · Coordinator Workflow Completion , Does the workflow data match the manual process or hack you documented in M2? Note whether the specific drop-offs or time gaps explain why your persona avoids the digital tool.:** Yeah, this is where the numbers and the interviews line up the hardest. My M2 workaround was the WhatsApp group standing in for the board on live updates, and the funnel shows exactly where that starts. 71% get through Route Assignment, then it falls off a cliff to 31% at Shift Handoff and just 18% at File Daily Report. That roughly 69% drop after assignment basically is the workaround showing up in the data. The moment they give up on the tool and switch to WhatsApp is the live-coordination and handoff stage, which is exactly what my persona described. The extra 4.2 minutes on Assign Routes (8.2 vs the 4.0 benchmark) is the cost of the reassignment lag I flagged in M2 (BUG-2044): she makes the change, doesn't trust it, and re-sends it over WhatsApp anyway. On the 5x Compliance number (14.6 vs 3 min), I'd actually push back on it being the first lever. It's the biggest single time sink and it's what's blowing up the 31-minute daily-waste figure, but Compliance is manager admin, not the real-time trust failure that spins up the parallel WhatsApp system and drives the churn. Fixing Compliance makes a task faster; fixing the trust gap wins back the account. So I'd call Compliance a good quick win, but the churn lever is the abandonment at handoff.

- **Snapshot 3 · Account Health & CSAT , Look at the CSAT heatmap. Which specific cell most directly maps to your persona's friction? Note how the NPS trend justifies the urgency of your M1 Problem Hook.:** I want to be careful here because the pivot-check trap is right in front of me. My persona's core loop, Core Dispatch, is actually her highest-rated cell (4.1, green). On the face of it that argues against my whole friction claim. The way I square it: the friction I found is a data-trust problem (stale board, lagging status), and a CSAT-by-feature score just can't catch that. It's a bug sitting inside a feature people otherwise like, so it never shows up as a red Core Dispatch cell. It shows up in behaviour (the Snapshot 2 abandonment) and in the account-level health instead. Coordinator NPS fell 30 points from +18 to -12, daily time lost tripled from about 9 to 31 minutes, and complexity-driven churn jumped from 1 in 8 accounts to 4 in 5. That trend is the real justification for my M1 hook; it's a structural shift, not a blip. On the red cells, Reporting (1.3) looks awful but it's a manager tool coordinators barely open (8% adoption), so it's low-leverage for my persona. Scheduling and AI just repeat the bloat story. I do buy the point that fixing the coordinator's board flows downstream to drivers too (accurate routes and status), but I'd hang the urgency on the NPS collapse and that 34% Velocity pilot result, not on any one CSAT cell.

