## Step 3 · Craft your hypothesis
- **Qualitative evidence (from M2) , quote the specific friction / moment of misery for your persona:** "We keep a WhatsApp group as the real system" (UXR-02), backed up by "A stop shows 'in progress' when it was delivered an hour ago, I can't trust the board" (UXR-09). Root causes: reassignments lag 8 to 15 minutes with no push (BUG-2044) and the dashboard status lags 20 to 60 minutes (BUG-2072).
- **Quantitative evidence (from M3) , name the metric or data point that confirms the pain; cite the number:** About 69% of coordinators drop the digital workflow right after Route Assignment (funnel: 71% assign, 31% shift handoff, 18% file report). Assign Routes takes 8.2 min against a 4.0 min benchmark. Daily time lost to workarounds has tripled, from about 9 to 31 minutes. Coordinator NPS fell 30 points, from +18 to -12. Complexity is now the churn reason for 4 of 5 accounts, up from 1 of 8. And the Velocity pilot is already showing route-assign speed up 34%.
- **Persona , role, goal, and the friction you confirmed in the reconciliation steps:** A mid-size 3PL fleet coordinator who just wants to run her whole shift off one board she can trust, reassigning routes and reading accurate stop status without a second phone in her hand. The friction I confirmed: she can't trust the board for live truth, so she bails on it right after route assignment and runs a parallel WhatsApp system all shift.
- **Problem you are solving , one sentence describing the specific friction this initiative removes:** RouteLogic Velocity closes the real-time trust gap in the core dispatch loop, so reassignments land instantly with confirmation and stop status matches what's actually happening on the ground, letting the coordinator run live coordination from one board instead of falling back to WhatsApp.
- **Strategic outcome , what behaviour change do you expect, and how does it map to retention / revenue / churn?:** Coordinators finish the dispatch workflow inside the product instead of bailing at handoff, the parallel WhatsApp habit fades, and the 31 minutes of daily waste shrinks. That change in behaviour brings back daily active usage and stickiness, which brings NPS back up, which means fewer enterprise accounts leaving over complexity. So it protects Net Revenue Retention. Retention, revenue and churn all move off the same trust fix.
- **Primary success metric (initiative signal) , the leading indicator that tells you the gap is closing:** Coordinator dispatch-workflow completion through Shift Handoff, moving from 31% up to at least 65% (in other words cutting the post-assignment drop-off from about 69% down to 35% or less). It's the earliest sign the board is being trusted again and coordination is coming back on-platform. Backup leading signals: daily time lost from 31 down to 15 minutes or less, and route-assign time from 8.2 down to 4.0 minutes or less.
- **Guardrail metric (product signal) , the metric that must NOT drop; it protects your existing base:** Coordinator adoption of the core loop, the Live Dispatch Board (91%) and Route Optimizer (85%), can't be allowed to slip, and Manager Reporting CSAT (4.5) and Compliance (3.9) can't drop either. That protects the backend enterprise value admins are paying for. Making the frontline simpler can't come at the cost of the platform power that keeps the contract signed.
- **Decision window , how much time or data before you scale, pivot, or kill? minimum threshold to proceed?:** A 90-day pilot across 2 or 3 enterprise accounts (one full retention cohort). To scale, I want post-assignment drop-off at 35% or less, daily time lost at 15 minutes or less, and no slip in core-feature adoption or manager CSAT, with Coordinator NPS heading back toward zero or positive. The pilot's early 34% jump in route-assign speed is a good sign. Go/no-go call at day 90: pivot if drop-off is still above 50%, kill if adoption or manager CSAT go backwards.
- **Draft your full hypothesis sentence , one to three sentences; quote the metric, name the persona, name the outcome:** Coordinators keep a parallel WhatsApp group because they can't trust the board (UXR-02, UXR-09), and the numbers back it up: about 69% drop the digital workflow right after route assignment, daily time lost to workarounds has tripled to 31 minutes, and Coordinator NPS has fallen 30 points to -12. My hypothesis is that restoring real-time trust in the core dispatch loop, meaning instant confirmed reassignments and stop status that matches what's happening on the ground, will pull live coordination back onto the platform for mid-size 3PL coordinators and cut that daily waste in half, which I'll measure by workflow completion (Open to Shift Handoff) climbing from 31% to at least 65%. I'll protect core-feature adoption (Dispatch Board 91%, Optimizer 85%) and manager Reporting CSAT (4.5) so a leaner frontline doesn't cost us the backend power buyers pay for, and I'll make the go/no-go call after a 90-day pilot on 2 or 3 accounts.
